<spell-page "json usage" notebook>
	<header level="1">
		JSON data
		<header>JSON data holds data in a page, as its text or from a file:  it draws nothing, and other elements read it.</>
	</>
	<section>
		<toolbar class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#from-script">From script</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-json>` holds JSON data in a page, and draws nothing.

			- Its text is the data, or `source` loads it from a file on the same site.
			- `load` says when:  `eager` (at once), `visible` or `idle`.
			- An element that takes JSON reads a `<ui-json>` child by itself.
			  `<ui-tree-diagram>` does, and follows it as it changes.

			A `<script type="application/json">` is the older way of holding data in a page.
			It works where it did, and inside a `<ui-json>` too.
			Use one there when the JSON holds a `<`, which a page would read as a tag.

			In Spell it's `<json>`, and its text is kept as written:
			its `{` and `[` are JSON's, not Spell's.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="From script">
		<markdown>
			`value` is the data, parsed.
			It's `undefined` while `source` loads, and when the text is empty or isn't JSON.
			Set it to hold other data.  `ui-changed` says when it changed.

			```js
			const data = document.querySelector("ui-json")
			await data.loaded
			console.log(data.value)

			data.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.value))
			data.value = { label: "New" }
			```

			Text that isn't JSON logs one console warning, sends `ui-error` and sets `:state(error)`.
			So does a file that won't load:  nothing draws, so nothing else would show it.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			JSON data has no role and shows nothing:  screen readers pass over it.
			What reads it says what it means:  a tree diagram names itself from the data.
		</>
	</>
</spell-page>
